<h1>ISFDB Bulk Data Procedures</h1>
<hr>

There have been a number of inquiries about merging private
databases with the ISFDB. This page describes how to go about
submitting your database to the ISFDB.

<hr>
<h1><a name="PREPARE">How to Prepare Your Data</a></h1>
Typically, a new database comes to me in one of the following 
formats:
<ol>
   <li> Flat text. The equivalent of typing up the Table of Contents
   and emailing it. This is the least preferred type of data, since it
   requires the most hand work. Should only be used to convey a
   limited number of magazines when no other method is possible.
   <li> Proprietary Database Format. This is some database format
   other than DBASE or ACCESS. To date, I have always been able to
   examine the binary contents of the file and figure out the data
   format. I've written a number of tools to extract this kind of 
   data. Better than the Flat Text format, but still error-prone.
   <li> DBASE or ACCESS format. I have already written tools to
   convert a DBASE file into a format I can work with. This format
   is almost always okay, unless some required data fields are missing.
   Everyone has a difference database layout, so conversion always
   requires writing a customized tool, which usually takes some time.
   <li> ISFDB format. Some people like to take the plunge and try
   to do some of thework for me by doing the conversions to ISFDB
   format themselves. Naturally, this is the best solution for me,
   since I can usually go directly to the data merging phase.
</ol>
The purpose of this document is to describe how to submit your data in
ISFDB format, either by directly creating the data in this format,
or by converting your current data into this format. The conversion
of databases in other formats usually requires some customized tools,
and remains beyond the scor of this document.

<h2><a name="PREPARE0">General Rules</a></h2>
No matter how you layout your database, there are three fields which are
absolutely required to merge your data into the ISFDB:
<ol>
   <li> The title of the work.
   <li> The author of the Work.
   <li> The year of publication.
</ol>
Everything else is gravy.  In addition, if you are submitting collection,
anthology, or magazine data, an additional field is needed: the publication key.
A publication key links a story to a magazine; it tells us where a story
appeared in print. I can create publication keys for some books like novels,
nonfiction, and non-genre works, but I can't guess which one of your collections
one of your short stories appeared in. More on publication keys later.

<h3><a name="PREPARE0A">Data Entry Rules</a></h3>
Most of the rules for data entry can be found in the FAQ or in the
online help page. A quick summary:
<ol>
   <li> Enter the title as-is; don't move leading articles to the end of
   the title. For instance, use "The Difference Engine", <b>not</b>
   "Difference Engine, The".
   <li> Enter authors as-is; don't enter them Lastname, Firstname. For instance,
   use "Robert A. Heinlein", <b>not</b> "Heinlein, Robert A.".
   <li> Use the "+" symbol to denote collaborations. For instance:
   "George R. R. Martin+Lisa Tuttle", <b>not</b>: "George R. R. Martin and Lisa Tuttle".
   <li> Do not remove the spaces between double-initialed authors. For
   instance, use "George R. R. Martin" or "H. P. Lovecraft", <b>not</b>
   "George R.R. Martin" or "H.P. Lovecraft".
   <li> If the author is unknown, enter an author of "unknown".
   <li> Don't guess the author, such as: "John Varley (??)". If you
   don't know the author use "unknown".
   <li> The year of the work must be an integer. Don't use "1938 (??)",
   or "circa 1921". If you don't know the year, use "0".
   <li> If the work is a famously unpublished work, use the year "8888"
   (For instance, <i>The Last Dangerous Visions</i>).
   <li> A work must belong to a series before it can belong to a super-series.
   <li> Do not include blank fields. For instance, if a novel does not
   belong to a series, don't include a blank series attribute.
   <li> The Cover Artist field should be treated just like the author field,
   so the same rules apply. The one difference: the artist name can be
   followed by the string <i>for "Story Name"</i>. This only applies to
   magazines which feature a story on the cover. For instance:
   <i>Paul Alexander for "The Barbie Murders"</i>.
</ol>
<h3><a name="PREPARE0B">Publication Keys or Tags</a></h3>
A publication key is a string which links a story to a particular
collection, anthology, or magazine. Since the key refers to a
specific publication, each book key must be unique.
<p>
The key can be any ascii string, although typically it is some
abbreviated version of the book's title and year. For instance,
I usually remove the vowels from a title, convert everything
to uppercase, and append the year of publication. For instance,
the publication key for <i>Rock of Ages</i> is: RCKFAGES1996.
The NESFA database used a couple of different methods. One
was to squish the title and precede it with the last two digits
of the year. <i>The Doris Lessing Reader</i> has a publication
tag of 88LessingReader. The other method was to use the initials
of the person entering the data, followed by the record number.
For instance, <i>Pleasant Dreams</i> has a publication key
of MLO4618.
<p>
If the concept of how a key or tag is used is still a little fuzzy, follow along
with this diagram:
<pre>
       title1     title2   title3       title4     title5
       TAG1       TAG1     TAG1,TAG2    TAG2       TAG2
        |          |        |    |       |          |
        +----------+--------+    +-------+----------+
        |                                |
       collection1                collection2
       TAG1                       TAG2
</pre>
title1, title2, and title3 were published in collection1.
collection1 has a publication tag of "TAG1". title1, title2,
and title3 show that they were published in collection1 by
having the TAG1 publication tag in their tag list.
<p>
title3, title4, and title5 were published in collection2.
collection2 has a publication tag of "TAG2". title3, title4,
and title5 show that they were published in collection2 by
having the TAG2 publication tag in their tag list.
<p>
Note that title3 was published in both collection1 and
collection2, so it has both TAG1 and TAG2 in its tag list.

<h3><a name="PREPARE0C">Capitalization Rules</a></h3>
About 95% of all data disagreements between the ISFDB and new
databases are differences in capitalization. There are a
number of reasons for the capitalization difficulties:
many titles, particularly short story titles, only appeared
in all-caps when published; the person entering the data 
altered the capitalization while typing in the information;
or the publisher didn't follow the traditional capitalization rules.
<p>
If you are confronted with an all-caps title and aren't
sure how to capitalize it, default to the following
rules: Capitalize all words unless
they are articles, prepositions, or conjunctions which are less
than 5 letters in length. The conjunctions include coordinating
conjunctions, subordinating conjunctions, and relative pronouns.
Note that the leading word of a title as well as all words which
follow a colon, hyphen, or slash should remain capitalized. 
<p>
So the list of words which should be lowercase are: a, also, an, 
and, at, but, by, for, from, if, in, into, near, next, nor, now, of,
off, on, once, onto, or, out, over, past, than, that, the, then,
thus, till, to, up, upon, what, when, who, with, yet. (Unless
they start the title or follow a ":" "-" "/") <b>Important Note:</b> If
the publisher capitalized the title improperly, please do <i>not</i>
correct it. Only use the capitalization rules if the title is
not capitalized at all.  Some titles do not follow the capitalization
rules; if the preferred capitalization is specified in the Table of 
Contents, use it. 

<h2><a name="PREPARE1">Data Format for Novels</a></h2>

Novels are probably the easiest of all the ISFDB data formats. Typically,
there are just two files involved: NOVELS and BOOKS. 
<p>
The NOVELS file lists novels without any regard to publication. It's
like a list of manuscripts that haven't been published: it contains data
about a novel that is not tied to any particular publication of the novel,
such as the novel's title, the author, copyright date, series information,
synopsis, and notes.
<p>
The BOOKS file lists all of the publication data for a specific printing
of a NOVEL. It contains publication data such as ISBN, price, page count,
the publisher, the publication format (hardcover, paperback, trade), and
the cover artist. It also contains information that might be found in the
NOVELS file, but can differ between print runs. For instance, the NOVELS
file will list the novel's first date of publication, the BOOKS file lists
the actual date of publication; the NOVELS file will list the actual
author of the novel, while the BOOKS file will list the author as it appears
on the title page (which may be a pseudonym); the NOVELS file will track
all of the variant titles that a novel may have had, while the BOOKS file
lists the exact title of that particular publication. The ISFDB database
format for the two files are:
<pre>

    -- NOVELS ------------------
    TITLE {
        AE=|AUTHOR|
        YR=|YEAR|
        SE=|SERIES|
        SS=|SUPER SERIES|
        SN=|SERIES NUMBER|
        PB=|ABV|
    }

    -- BOOKS ------------------
    TITLE {
	AB=|ABV|
	AE=|AUTHOR|
	YR=|YEAR|
	PB=|PUB|
	IS=|ISBN|
	PR=|$PRICE|
	PP=|PAGES|
	TP=|FORMAT|
	CV=|ARTIST|
    }
</pre>
<p>
Why the split between the NOVELS and BOOKS information? Because a book is 
usually written only once (although it may be revised a few times), while
it may be published numerous times.  The NOVEL entry points at it's
publication records through the PB field (which is the publication tag
or key), which points to the AB field of the associated BOOKS entry.
<p>
For example, here is the novel entry for <i>Hyperion</i> and two of
it's publication records:
<pre>

    -- NOVELS ------------------
    Hyperion {
        AE=|Dan Simmons|
        YR=|1989|
        SE=|Hyperion Cantos|
        SN=|1|
        PB=|HYPE1989A,HYPE1989B|
    }

    -- BOOKS ------------------
    Hyperion {
        AB=|HYPE1989A|
        AE=|Dan Simmons|
        YR=|1989|
        PB=|Bantam Spectra|
        IS=|0-553-28368-5|
        PP=|482|
        TP=|pb|
        PR=|$5.99|
        CV=|Gary Ruddell|
    }
    Hyperion {
        AB=|HYPE1989B|
        AE=|Dan Simmons|
        YR=|1989|
        PB=|Doubleday|
        IS=|0-385-24949-7|
        PP=|481|
        TP=|hc|
        PR=|$18.95|
    }
</pre>

<h3><a name="PREPARE1A">Introductions, Forwards, and Prefaces</a></h3>
If you wish to be <i>really</i> complete, you may want to add data
on introductions, forwards, and other nonfiction material written
by the author or someone else, and place either before or after the novel.
At present, this type of data is
place in the essays/articles area of the database. The entry should
be listed under the file "ESSAYS". Here's an example of how an afterword
could be added to <i>Foundation's Edge</i>:

<pre>

    -- NOVELS ------------------
    Foundation's Edge {
        AE=|Isaac Asimov|
        YR=|1982|
        SE=|Foundation|
        PB=|FNDTNSDG1982|
    }

    -- ESSAYS ------------------
    Afterword by the Author {
        AE=|Isaac Asimov|
        YR=|1982|
        PB=|FNDTNSDG1982|
    }

    -- BOOKS ------------------
    Foundation's Edge {
        AB=|FNDTNSDG1982|
        AE=|Isaac Asimov|
        YR=|1982|
	PB=|Doubleday|
	IS=|SFBC|
	PP=|366|
	TP=|hc|
	CV=|Joe Caroff|
}
</pre>

<h3><a name="PREPARE1B">The Omnibus</a></h3>
An omnibus is a special publication, usually offered as a Book Club
edition, containing 2 or more novels. An omnibus is almost always
part of a series, so it should be placed in the same series as the
novels that are contained within the omnibus. Omnibus entries are
placed in the file called OMNIBUS. The omnibus data is entered just
like a novel. The only unusual thing that occurs is the publication
tag for the omnibus is put in the omnibus entry as well as each novel
that appears in the omnibus. As an example, here is the
omnibus for Walter Jon Williams' Maijstral series, with publication
data for both the omnibus and each novel's previous publications:
<pre>

    -- OMNIBUS ----------------
    Ten Points For Style {
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1995|
        PB=|TPFS1995|
        SE=|Maijstral|
    }

    -- NOVELS -----------------
    The Crown Jewels {
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1987|
        SE=|Maijstral|
        SN=|1|
        PB=|CRJW1987,TPFS1995|
    }
    House of Shards {
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1988|
        SE=|Maijstral|
        SN=|2|
        PB=|HOSH1988,TPFS1995|
    }
    Rock of Ages {
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1995|
        SE=|Maijstral|
        SN=|3|
        PB=|TPFS1995,RCKFAGES1996|
    }

    -- BOOKS ------------------
    The Crown Jewels {
        AB=|CRJW1987|
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1987|
        PB=|Tor|
        PP=|247|
        TP=|pb|
        PR=|$3.50|
        IS=|0-812-55798-0|
    }
    House of Shards {
        AB=|HOSH1988|
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        IS=|0-812-55783-2|
        YR=|1988|
        PB=|Tor|
        PP=|309|
        PR=|3.95|
        TP=|pb|
        CV=|Don Brautigan|
    }
    Rock of Ages {
        AB=|RCKFAGES1996|
        AE=|Walter Jon Williams|
        YR=|1996|
        CV=|Romas|
        PB=|Tor|
        IS=|0-812-51382-7|
        PP=|287|
        PR=|$5.99|
        TP=|pb|
    }
    Ten Points For Style {
        AB=|TPFS1995|
