<h1><a name="RECEIVED">What Happens To Your Data</a></h2>

Okay; you give me your data, and three weeks later it still hasn't shown
up in the ISFDB. What gives? As it turns out, merging your data into the
ISFDB isn't just a matter of dropping the new data on the disk and letting 
her rip. Duplicates have to be merged. Some of the duplicates will have 
slightly different titles (due to typos) and be difficult to find. Your 
data may think that "Collection X" by "Author Z" has 5 short stories, 
while the ISFDB claims that it has 6. Your data may claim that some novel
has 162 pages while the ISFDB has "xii+162" (preface).
<p>
The following paragraphs describe, step-by-step,
exactly what is done to the data before it is integrated into the ISFDB.
This section started as a place to write down the procedures as they
were being developed; now it's grown into a kind of monograph about the
arcane science of merging fiction databases. They are the steps that
anyone needs to take when merging any two fiction databases together.
As it turns out, almost all of the merging work centers around finding
errors in the two databases.

<h2><a name="RECEIVED1">Checking the Veracity of the New Data</a></h2>
The first step in merging the new data is to check for meta-errors. This
phase isn't concerned with incorrect data, but rather with
missing and mangled data.  In order to check the new database for lexical 
inconsistencies, certain fields in the new database are required. For 
instance, it is impossible to determine whether the following two stories:

<pre>
	"The Database on the Edge of Forever" by Cordwainer Ellison
	"The Data on the Edge of Forever" by ??
</pre>

are actually the same story. A search of the standard references for the
second record can be made by title only. If the second record is the errant
one, it will not show up in any index; since the author is missing,
a search by author name cannot be performed. But all indexes are 
incomplete, so a missing title is no guarantee that the title is bogus.
So the veracity tool checks the following:
<ol>
  <li> If the database files are constructed from a proprietary format,
  the field which determines that a record is a novel, collections,
  anthology, magazine, etc.. is checked. Bad values are flagged and
  researched.
  <li> Each collection and anthology record is checked for
  missing author/editor data. If the work is a collection, the author 
  can be determined by just looking at the author of the story data that
  points to it.  If the work is an anthology, the editor can be found
  by going back to the primary source, or by looking up the data in a 
  secondary source. If no editor is found, the editor is made "unknown".
  Every novel, collection, and anthology <i>will</i> have an author/editor.
  <li> Each record is searched for missing year data. The second pass
  of the lexical inconsistency tools (a later phase in the merging process)
  looks for similar titles with 
  identical years; the more accurate the year field, the more accurate
  the lexical search will be. Unknown years are given the value "0".
  Note that, in the example above, if the year of publication were
  present in both records:
<pre>
	"The Database on the Edge of Forever" (1972)
	"The Data on the Edge of Forever" (1972)
</pre>
  The lexical tool <i>would</i> find this error during the second pass
  through the data (and since the 2nd record is incomplete, it's accuracy
  would be suspect).
  <li> If the database contains story data, each of the stories is
  checked to make sure that its key (or tag) refers to a publication
  that actually resides in the database. Bad references are flagged.
  <li> If the database contains story data, each publication is checked
  to make sure that some story actually refers to it.
</ol>
<b>Note:</b> <i>Once a list of meta-errors has been collected, you may
be asked to help resolve these errors, since you probably have access
to the primary sources.</i> After all, the data came from <i>your</i> database.
<p>
<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED2">Scanning for Lexical Inconsistencies</a></h2>
Okay, you're probably asking "What, <i>exactly</i>, is a lexical
inconsistency?" The answer: 
<ol>
  <li> If both titles are "similar" and both authors are "similar", or
  <li> If both "titles" are similar and both years are identical,
  <li> And, the titles and authors are not identical, then
  <li> the records are <i>possibly</i> inconsistent.
</ol>
Okay, now you're probably asking "What, <i>exactly</i>, is "similar"?"
"Similar" is defined as:
<ol>
  <li>If two target strings are identical, then they also similar.
  <li>If two target strings are reduced to lowercase, and they are
      then identical, then they are similar. Example:
<pre>
	"Children Of The Centaur" = "children of the centaur"
	"Children of the Centaur" = "children of the centaur"
</pre>
     are similar, but not identical.
  <li>If two target strings are reduced to lowercase, and the spaces
      are removed, and they are then identical, then they are similar.
      Example:
<pre>
	"H. P. Lovecraft" = "h.p.lovecraft"
	"H.P. Lovecraft"  = "h.p.lovecraft"
</pre>
  <li>Two target strings are reduced to lowercase, and the spaces
     are removed. The strings are then compared by position from front to end,
     counting the number of matching characters. The strings are
     then compared from end to front, counting the number of 
     matching characters. A ratio of character matches to the longest string
     length is then made (perfectly identical strings have a ratio
     of 2.0). If the ratio is greater than 0.85, the strings are
     considered similar. Example:
<pre>
	Forward Pass:
	"The Shobies Story"  = "theshobiesstory"
	"The Shobies' Story" = "theshobies'story"
	                        ^^^^^^^^^^        Matches on 10 characters

	Backward Pass:
	"The Shobies Story"  =  "theshobiesstory"
	"The Shobies' Story" = "theshobies'story"
	                                   ^^^^^  Matches on 5 characters

	Ratio = (10+5)/16 = 0.9375 = similar
</pre>
     Why 0.85? This value was found empirically by feeding 
     known problem authors and titles into the tool. The intention of
     this rule is to find minor typographic insertions or deletions.
<p>
  <li>If the target strings contain multiple data items (for instance,
     the author field may list multiple authors for a collaboration,
     or the title field may list multiple variant titles), then the
     possibility exists that the data items are presented in a different
     order in the two target strings. In this case, the above definitions
     of "similar" may not detect truly similar strings. In the ISFDB,
     if the target strings contain either the "+" character (collaboration)
     or the "^" character (variant title or pseudonym), the strings are
     decomposed into substrings using those characters as delimeters. Each
     substring from one target string is compared to each substring of the
     other target string. If any substrings are similar (in the sense of
     similar[1] through similar[4] above), the strings are similar. 
<p>
     An example collaboration citation, where string [2] is missing the
     collaboration:
<pre>
     [1] = "Jerry Pournelle+Larry Niven"   = "jerrypournelle" +
	                                     "larryniven"

     [2] = "Larry Niven"                   = "larryniven"
</pre>
     Normally, strings [1] and [2] would not be considered similar. String
     [1] has been decomposed into "jerrypournelle" and "larryniven" 
     substrings. Each will be compared to string [2]:
<pre>
     "jerrypournelle" vs "larryniven"  = (NOT SIMILAR)
     "larryniven" vs "larryniven"      = (SIMILAR) **
</pre>
<p>
     Hence, strings [1] and [2] are similar.  Strings with "^" characters 
     are used to denote a pseudonym or variant title.  An example
     of a missing pseudonym citation:
<pre>
     [1] = "S. P. Somtow^Somtow Sucharitkul"  = "s.p.somtow" +
				                "somtowsucharitkul"

     [2] = "Somtow Sucharitkul"               = "somtowsucharitkul"

     "s.p.somtow" vs "somtowsucharitkul"        = (NOT SIMILAR)
     "somtowsucharitkul" vs "somtowsucharitkul" = (SIMILAR) **
</pre>
     And the variant title case:
<pre>
     [1] = "The Computer Connection^The Indian Giver^Extro"
				    = "thecomputerconnection"
				    = "theindiangiver"
				    = "extro"

     [2] = "Extro^Computer Connection"
				    = "extro"
				    = "computerconnection"

     "thecomputerconnection" vs "extro"              = (NOT SIMILAR)
     "thecomputerconnection" vs "computerconnection" = (SIMILAR) **
     "theindiangiver"        vs "extro"              = (NOT SIMILAR)
     "theindiangiver"        vs "computerconnection" = (NOT SIMILAR)
     "extro"                 vs "extro"              = (SIMILAR) **
     "extro"                 vs "computerconnection" = (NOT SIMILAR)
</pre>
</ol>
<p>
Note that the above rules will not find every error. For instance, the
current rules are not powerful enough to detect that these titles:
<pre>
	"The Private War of Pvt. Jacob"
	"The Private War of Private Jacob"
</pre>
<p>
are, in fact, similar (they generate a similarity ratio of 0.81). The target
similarity ratio could be reduced to find more titles, but the number of
false positives begins to grow overwhelmingly. Every similarity hit must
be looked at by hand, so reducing the number of false positives is important.
The other fact to keep in mind is that in a later phase, the content of
collections, anthologies, and magazines will be compared, and that these
comparisons are done by exact title. As long as the two databases share 
the book the story was published in, the error will be detected.
<p>
Now that you know what a "lexical inconsistency" is, and what "similar"
means, we can discuss the tool itself.
<p>
The lexical inconsistency tool scans the new database and creates a list 
of title/author/year triplets. On the first pass, each title/author pair
in the database is checked against every other title/author pair in the database. If 
two pairs are similar, but not identical, the entries are flagged.  On
the second pass, each title/year pair is checked against every other
title/year pair in the new database. If the titles are similar and the
years are identical, the entries are flagged. Each flagged entry is then
examined by hand. 
<p>
Note that, at this phase in the merging process, nothing has yet been
compared to the ISFDB; these are all internal inconsistencies in your
database.
<i>Once a list of lexical inconsistencies has been collected,
you may be asked to help resolve these errors, since you probably have 
access to the primary sources.</i> 

<p>
<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED3">Collapsing the Records</a></h2>

Most bibliographic databases have a separate record for each short
story publication. That is, if a short story appears in a magazine,
an anthology, and a collection, there will be three separate short
story records for that title:
<pre>
	Bears Discover Fire {
       		AE=|Terry Bisson|
       		YR=|1990|
        	SL=|ss|
        	PB=|ASFAUG90|
	}
	Bears Discover Fire {
       		AE=|Terry Bisson|
       		YR=|1992|
        	PB=|NEAW1992B|
	}
	Bears Discover Fire {
       		AE=|Terry Bisson|
       		YR=|1993|
        	PB=|BRDF1993|
	}
</pre>

The ISFDB, on the other hand, displays a <i>summary</i> of an author's
works, so we only want a single title to be displayed on the author's
Summary Bibliography page. In order to reduce the amount of computation
done at run-time, the three records above are collapsed into the single 
record:

<pre>
	Bears Discover Fire {
       		AE=|Terry Bisson|
       		YR=|1990|
        	SL=|ss|
        	PB=|ASFAUG90,NEAW1992B,BRDF1993|
	}
</pre>

While this result is completely machine-generated, it does require
an exact title/author match. This explains the importance of the
previous lexical inconsistency search.

<p>
<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED4">Finding External Lexical Inconsistencies</a></h2>

Now that both databases are in ISFDB format, and both databases are
supposedly internally consistent, we can now start the process of
merging the databases by looking for lexical inconsistencies between
the two databases.
<p>
When the actual merging begins, there are four possible relationships
that the data can have between the two databases:
<ol>
  <li> The book in the new database is not present in the ISFDB. In
  this case, the data can simply be inserted wholesale.
  <li> The book is known to the ISFDB, but only by title. No publication
  data or content information is known to the ISFDB. In this case, the
  publication and content data can simply be inserted, <i>but</i> the
  databases have to be in agreement on the title/author of the book. 
  Title typos will result in the introduction of a duplicate lexical variant.
  <li> The book is known to the ISFDB, and both databases are in complete
  agreement. In this case, the new data is tossed, as the ISFDB already
  has it. The title/authors of everything must be in agreement for this
  to occur.
  <li> The book is known to the ISFDB, but there is a disagreement over
  publication data or content information. Naturally, this is where all
  the merging work occurs.
</ol>
So once again, ensuring the integrity of the titles is the first order
of business. The process for external lexical inconsistencies is the
nearly the same as finding internal ones, with one small difference:
only differences <i>between</i> the databases are reported. Internal
inconsistencies are ignored, so it is critical to go through the
internal inconsistency phase prior to moving on to this one. 
<p>
For example, say each database has two records for "Title 1". Both
databases have a correct record and an incorrect one (internal inconsistency):
<pre>
	ISFDB: "Title 1" and "Titel 1"
	NEWDB: "Title 1" and "title 1"
</pre>
If the internal inconsistencies are not removed first, then the external
inconsistency phase will find the following errors:
<pre>
	"Title 1" (ISFDB)  vs  "title 1" (NEWDB)
	"Titel 1" (ISFDB)  vs  "Title 1" (NEWDB)
	"Titel 1" (ISFDB)  vs  "title 1" (NEWDB)
</pre>
Whereas, if the databases were internally consistent, there would have
been no errors found at all, saving a great deal of time.
<p>
After the external inconsistencies have been resolved, both the ISFDB
and the NEWDB are collapsed again to pick up any duplicate titles that
might have been introduced.
<p>
<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED5">Putting Publication Data Into Agreement</a></h2>

The first step in merging the publication data is ensuring that there are
no internal publication duplicates. An internal duplicate basically means
that the same book has been mistakenly entered twice into the database.
Duplicates in the NEWDB will have been found in an earlier merging phase.
Duplicates in the ISFDB can be found be running the "pubint" utility. 
<p>
Once the duplicates have been removed, we can search for external publication
data differences between the two databases. So far, we have made sure
that the databases agree on story titles, book titles, and who wrote
what. Now we want the databases to agree on ISBN's, publication houses, page
count, etc...  Title matches must be identical during this phase, so the
databases must be lexically consistent at this point. The simplified
algorithm for finding these differences is:
<pre>
   For every book entry in the NEWDB
      Find the matching book entry in the ISFDB
      If a match exists
         Extract pub keys from the NEWDB 
         Extract pub keys from the ISFDB 
         For every NEWDB pub key
            For every ISFDB pub key
	       Extract the pub data using the NEWDB key
	       Extract the pub data using the ISFDB key
	       Compare NEWDB pub data to ISFDB pub data
	       If differences
	          Output differences
               Else
	          Emit a NEWDB:ISFDB key mapping
</pre>
As an example, let's say the NEWDB has a set of collections (a book
of short fiction by a single author):
<ol> 
   <li> Every collection in the NEWDB is examined.
   <li> The ISFDB is searched to see if a collection with a matching
	title and author name is found.
   <li> If there is no such entry in the ISFDB, the NEWDB collection
	must be new (since all lexical inconsistencies have already been
	resolved). New entries will be added to the ISFDB in a
	subsequent merging phase.
   <li> If there was a matching entry in the ISFDB, then prepare
	to compare publication data.
   <li> Extract the publication keys from the NEWDB collection entry. This is
	the field that connects a short stories to a particular publication.
   <li> Extract the publication keys from the ISFDB collection entry.
   <li> For each NEWDB publication key, find the associated publication
	data.
   <li> For each ISFDB publication key, find the associated publication
	data.
   <li> Compare each NEWDB publication field of one publication record
	to the associated publication field of one ISFDB publication
	record.
   <li> If a difference exists, there may be a problem.
   <li> If no differences exist, then the NEWDB and the ISFDB are in agreement.
	Output a key mapping. This states: for NEWDB publication key X, the 
	corresponding ISFDB publication key is Y. This mapping list describes
	the books which intersect both the NEWDB and the ISFDB.
</ol>
After modifying the databases, the above algorithm is repeated. This
cycle continues until all discrepancies have been addressed, or have been
dismissed as irrelevant.  Once this phase is complete, the keymap should
describe those books which are common to both the ISFDB and the NEWDB;
all further work will be based on this keymap instead of title/author string matches.
<p>
<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED6">Putting Content Into Agreement</a></h2>

For books which intersect both the ISFDB and the new database,
there is now agreement on book titles, author names, and publication
data. Now we need to put the two databases into agreement concerning
content. When this phase is complete, a book will either be completely
and accurately represented in the ISFDB, or it will be completely new
data which can simply be tossed into the ISFDB as-is.
<pre>
	For each entry in the keymap
	   Extract all data associated with the NEWDB key
	   Get the associated ISFDB key from the keymap
	   Extract all data associated with the ISFDB key
	   For each data record in the NEWDB
	       Find the related ISFDB record
	       If no record exists
		  Emit an NEWDB->ISFDB error
               Else
		  Mark the record as verified
	   For each unverified data record in the ISFDB
	      Emit an ISFDB->NEWDB error
</pre>

<hr>
<h2><a name="RECEIVED7">Merging Non-intersecting Data from the NEWDB into ISFDB</a></h2>
At this point, all data in the NEWDB which has a key that is not
in the keymap should be moved into the ISFDB. The first step in this
process is to cull the data associated with keys that are in the keymap.
The algorithm for this is:
<pre>
	For each entry in the keymap
	   Search all data records
	   If the key is contained in a record's keylist
	      Remove the key
           If the record's keylist is now empty
	      Mark the record DONTUSE
        For each record in the NEWDB
	   If the record is not marked DONTUSE
	      Emit the record's data appropriately
</pre>
This will created a new set of NEWDB files. Then:
<pre>
      For each non-empty file in the NEWDB, concatenate the file
        with the associated ISFDB file
      Collapse every touched file in the ISFDB
      As a final check, look for internal inconsistencies in the
	affected ISFDB files
</pre>
At this point, the merging is officially complete.

